 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Gefühlsfarben is the German word for “emotional colors.” A more poetic way of putting it might be “colors that are felt as much as seen.” Albert Kriemler often partners with artists for his Akris collections, turning photographs into prints and paintings into embroideries. This season he was drawn to the work of the Austrian artist Martha Jungwirth. “What I liked the most is how she said, ‘When I’m painting, my process guides me to my creativity,’” he remarked backstage.</p><p>Kriemler has honed a refined sense of chic over the years at Akris, though he rejects the descriptor minimal. The word hardly fits for this collection. Process was apparent in the finished products, from the shaggy underside of jacquards modeled on a Jungwirth painting he used for a sleeveless trench and blouse to the ribbon-like embroidery that evoked another of her canvases on a fluttery dress. Jungwirth is an abstract artist—“a blotch is a blotch is a blotch,” she once said—and Kriemler’s adaptations of her work did justice to her energetic gestural brushstrokes without being literal, an impressive achievement.</p><p>He also took inspiration from her vibrant color palette. Red, pink, and purple, by Jungwirth’s reckoning, are the feeling colors, while green stands for nature and yellow for light. They’re cheering hues, impossible to miss, though a series of black looks were not to be ignored.\nKriemler added a pair of pockets to the hips of a sleeveless sheath to create an appealing hourglass shape. It was as rigorous as the summery plissé belted number that followed a couple of looks later was generous. The collection’s most impressive piece was a strapless gown made from hundreds—possibly thousands—of little rectangular strips of silk that had been printed with one of Jungwirth’s paintings and stitched to a base on just one of the rectangles’ short sides. It vibrated hypnotically with the model’s every step.</p></div>",
